.TH WORDFREQ 1
.SH NAME
wordfreq \- count words, most frequent first
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wordfreq
[
.B -b
]
[
.B -n
.I count
]
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wordfreq
reads
.I file
(standard input if none given), splits it into words - a word is a
maximal run of the letters
.BR a - z
and
.BR A - Z ,
anything else is a separator, so case is kept and a possessive like
.B mountain's
splits into
.B mountain
and
.BR s ,
same as
.IR tr (1)'s
.B -cs A-Za-z
would - and prints each distinct word with its count, most frequent
first, ties broken by the word itself so the output is the same from one
run to the next.
.TP
.B -b
Count adjacent word pairs (bigrams) instead of single words: each output
line is a count followed by the two words, space-separated.
.TP
.BI -n " count"
Print only the
.I count
most frequent entries.
.SH EXAMPLE
.TP
.EX
wordfreq -n 10 genesis
wordfreq -b -n 10 genesis
.EE
.IP
The ten most common words, then the ten most common word pairs.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/wordfreq.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sort (1),
.IR uniq (1),
.IR tr (1),
.IR sh-regex (1)
.SH BUGS
This is
.B tr -cs A-Za-z | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
- and, with
.BR -b ,
the bigram pipeline built from
.IR paste (1)
and
.IR tail (1)
besides - as one program rather than four processes and three pipes.
Written for
.IR lib/demo/inferno-for-dante.sh ,
which uses no
.IR tr ,
.IR sort ,
.IR uniq ,
.IR grep
or
.I sed
at all: this is the part of that exercise which genuinely needs a real
program instead of a shell one-liner - counting and sorting a whole
corpus - where
.I sh-regex
(1)'s
.B match
and
.B ${re ...}
cover the filtering and substitution natively instead.
